## §9 System design — the S1 emission path under P1

This is the DESIGN-stage deliverable: the component graph, interfaces, data flow, and state
machine for the emitter that P1 requires. It is design only. It is scoped to P1 and P3
(inside PAEOS's boundary); P2's new fields appear as declared nulls until ratified.

### §9.1 Component graph

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph PAEOS["PAEOS boundary — single writer (K8/I9)"]
        SEAL["kernel/seal.py — SealAuthority\n(existing) emits SealRecord"]
        L15["L15 EmissionWriter (NEW)\npost-seal, in-repo"]
        COLL["StageArtifactCollector (NEW)\nreads live stage objects, not markdown"]
        MLOG["L17 MistakeLogWriter (NEW, P3)\nOutcome + decision binding"]
        OUTBOX[("ledger/emissions/\nappend-only outbox")]
    end

    subgraph SAKG["SAKG boundary — separate writer"]
        PULL["IngestReader\npull only, never push"]
        VAL["BundleValidator\nrejects incomplete bundles"]
        MINT["NodeMinter\ndeterministic id from natural key"]
        ENV["EnvelopeCompleter\nfills provenance; declares nulls"]
        STORE[("CandidateStore\nstatus = candidate")]
    end

    SEAL -->|SealRecord| L15
    COLL -->|typed stage objects| L15
    L15 -->|EmissionBundle v1| OUTBOX
    MLOG -->|OutcomeRecord| OUTBOX
    OUTBOX -.->|pull, read-only| PULL
    PULL --> VAL --> MINT --> ENV --> STORE

    classDef gap stroke-dasharray: 5 5
    class MLOG,COLL gap
```

**Boundary rule (Ω10, K8).** The outbox is the only shared surface. PAEOS writes it and never
reads SAKG; SAKG reads it and never writes PAEOS. The arrow into `PULL` is dashed because it
crosses a trust boundary: everything past it is untrusted data (T9, FPL-7).

**No orphan.** Every component has an inbound producer and an outbound consumer. `COLL` and
`MLOG` are dashed because they do not exist yet — they are exactly what P1 and P3 create.

### §9.2 Interfaces

Language-neutral (PAEOS-7.6 §1 notation).

```
# --- PAEOS side (NEW) ---

EmissionWriter.emit(seal: SealRecord, artifacts: StageArtifactSet) -> BundleRef
    # Purity:      side-effect confined to one append to the outbox.
    # Ordering:    strictly post-seal. MUST NOT be reachable from any pre-seal stage,
    #              so nothing SAKG does can affect the goal that produced it (§6.1b r1).
    # Idempotence: emit(s, a) twice yields the same BundleRef. Keyed on seal.seal_hash.
    # Failure:     a bundle that fails its own schema is NOT written. Emission never
    #              degrades a sealed goal — it fails closed and logs.

StageArtifactCollector.collect(goal_id: GoalId, run_id: RunId) -> StageArtifactSet
    # Reads live typed objects from the stage record, never re-parses markdown.
    # This is the whole of P1.

MistakeLogWriter.record(goal_id, subject_decision: NodeKey, entries: [Attempt]) -> OutcomeRecord
    # P3. `subject_decision` is the F6 binding key; it is REQUIRED, not optional.

# --- The wire format ---

EmissionBundle:
    schema_version:   "sakg-emission/1"
    seal_hash:        Hash          # from SealRecord — the natural key
    goal_id:          GoalId
    run_id:           RunId
    namespace:        ProjectRef
    emitted_at:       Ts
    nodes:            [EmissionNode]
    edges:            [EmissionEdge]
    declared_absent:  {field: reason_code}   # P2 gap, made visible (A5 discipline)

EmissionNode:
    natural_key:      str           # stable: "<goal_id>/<type>/<ordinal>" — NOT a ULID
    type:             NodeType      # one of §4.3, assigned by the producer, not inferred
    label:            str
    summary:          str           # <= 280, authored at emission
    evidence:         [Hash]        # artifact_bundle / verdict_ref / adversary_ref
    constitutional_basis: [ClauseRef] | null   # null + reason until DEBT-0004 closes

# --- SAKG side ---

IngestReader.pull(since: BundleRef | null) -> [EmissionBundle]      # read-only
BundleValidator.validate(b: EmissionBundle) -> Valid | Rejected(reasons)
NodeMinter.mint(namespace, natural_key) -> NodeId                    # deterministic
EnvelopeCompleter.complete(node, bundle) -> Node                     # §4.2 envelope
```

**The three interface decisions, and why.**

1. **`natural_key`, not ULID.** §4.2's ULID is non-deterministic, and §4.2 also forbids
   deletion — together they guarantee duplicate nodes on any re-ingest. A producer-assigned
   natural key makes `NodeMinter.mint` a pure function and makes P1's exit test ("re-ingest
   twice, zero new nodes") checkable. It is also the key F6 needs.
2. **`type` assigned by the producer.** The producer knows what it built; the ingester would
   be guessing from prose. This moves the §2.1-row-2 judgment call from SAKG to PAEOS, where
   the information actually is.
3. **`declared_absent` is mandatory, not optional.** Until P2 ratifies, `falsifier`,
   `valid_until`, `coordinates`, and `facets` have no author. Emitting them as explicit,
   reason-coded nulls keeps the gap countable instead of letting it read as "no data yet."
   This is A5's `omitted: n` discipline applied to the ingest path.

### §9.3 Data flow

```
  goal reaches L16 and seals
        │
        ▼
  SealRecord{seal_hash, goal_id, run_id, artifact_bundle,
             verdict_ref, adversary_ref, attestation}
        │                                    ┌─ StageArtifactSet (live typed objects)
        ▼                                    ▼
  EmissionWriter.emit ─────────────► EmissionBundle v1 ──► append to outbox
        │                                                        │
  (later, on attribution)                                        │
  MistakeLogWriter.record ──► OutcomeRecord{subject_decision} ────┤
                                                                 │
                                          ═══ TRUST BOUNDARY ═══ │
                                                                 ▼
                                            IngestReader.pull (read-only)
                                                                 │
                                      ┌──────────────────────────┤
                                      ▼                          ▼
                            BundleValidator            Rejected → quarantine,
                                      │                 never partially ingested
                                      ▼
                            NodeMinter (deterministic)
                                      ▼
                            EnvelopeCompleter
                                  │  provenance.origin   ← goal_id + run_id
                                  │  provenance.source_hash ← seal_hash
                                  │  valid_from          ← emitted_at
                                  │  status              ← candidate  (always)
                                  │  confidence          ← DERIVED, never copied
                                  │  declared_absent     ← preserved verbatim
                                  ▼
                            CandidateStore
```

**Every element is produced and consumed.** `attestation` and `ledger_head` from the
`SealRecord` are deliberately *not* forwarded: they are PAEOS-internal integrity data, and
§3.2.3 forbids copying evidence across the boundary. Only hashes cross (§6.1b rule 2).

### §9.4 State machine — bundle and node lifecycle

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> SEALED: kernel seal committed
    SEALED --> EMITTED: EmissionWriter.emit succeeds
    SEALED --> EMIT_FAILED: bundle fails own schema
    EMIT_FAILED --> [*]: logged; goal unaffected (fails closed)

    EMITTED --> PULLED: IngestReader.pull
    PULLED --> REJECTED: BundleValidator fails
    REJECTED --> [*]: quarantined whole; never partially ingested
    PULLED --> CANDIDATE: validated, minted, envelope completed

    CANDIDATE --> CANONICAL: evidence binding observed (x5)
    CANDIDATE --> SUPERSEDED: a later bundle supersedes this natural_key
    CANONICAL --> SUPERSEDED: supersedes edge
    CANONICAL --> REFUTED: falsifier observed
    CANDIDATE --> REFUTED: falsifier observed
    SUPERSEDED --> [*]: retained, never deleted
    REFUTED --> [*]: retained — a refuted decision is high-value (FPL-2)

    note right of CANDIDATE
      Frequency may raise review
      priority. It may never cause
      the CANONICAL transition (x5).
    end note

    note right of REFUTED
      Unreachable until P2 ratifies:
      no PAEOS stage authors
      `falsifier` today (F4).
      Modeled, not yet live.
    end note
```

**Every state has an entry and an exit.** `REFUTED` and the `falsifier` guard are modeled but
**dead until P2** — declared here rather than omitted, because an unreachable transition that
nobody wrote down is how F4 arose in the first place.

