## §11 Perspectives covered · no orphan / no gap

**Perspectives (multi-perspective critique input, L09).**

- **Falsification.** The probe was built so it *could* return GO: three goals spanning thin to
  rich, a control chosen because it was the best case in the repo, and two robustness
  recomputations that each had a real chance of overturning the headline. It returned NO-GO on
  six boolean observations, not on a scalar. ✔
- **Fairness to the design.** §6.2a rescores against §6.1b's own seven-type bundle rather than
  the full twelve; §1.3 states the objection up front; §7.1 records everything that survived,
  including the result most at risk (rejected Options, 3/3). ✔
- **Constitutional.** The verdict recommends; the founder legislates (CER-5). P2 is routed to
  CER-2 as an IP rather than decided here. No new mechanism was introduced — §9 composes the
  existing `SealAuthority` and outbox concepts (CER-6). ✔
- **Security / trust boundary.** §9.1's outbox preserves K8 single-writer discipline across the
  boundary (Ω10); only hashes cross (§6.1b r2, §3.3.3); emission is strictly post-seal, so FPL-3
  non-authority holds by construction — nothing on this path can affect the goal that produced it. ✔
- **Blast radius (A-2).** This artifact is DESIGN only: no code, no kernel change, no
  constitutional change. P1/P3 are `runtime/`-scope (expected SOFT). P2 is a governed PAEOS
  artifact change and is explicitly *not* taken here. ✔
- **Economy (FPL-4).** The probe cost one session and returns a decision that §8's ordering
  would have spent a full slice to reach. ✔
- **The opposing case, stated.** The strongest argument for GO is that 0.59 recovery of a
  never-before-populated graph is a good first slice, and that Ω1's core claim — the exhaust is
  rich — held. §8.2 concedes both. It fails anyway on F2/F3/F6: two of the three sources
  §6.1b names do not exist, and the FPL-2 binding cannot be formed. You cannot ship a
  longitudinal memory whose longitudinal edge has no key. ✔

**No orphan / no gap.**

- Every §4.2 envelope field is scored in every goal (16 × 3), with `confidence` excluded under
  a stated, verdict-invariant rationale (A6).
- Every §4.3 node type is scored in every goal (12 × 3). No type is skipped.
- Every §6.1b bundle source is checked, and each of the seven is either confirmed or falsified
  (F1, F2, F3).
- Every §9.1 component has a producer and a consumer; the two that do not yet exist are marked
  dashed and mapped to P1 and P3.
- Every §9.3 data element is produced and consumed; the two `SealRecord` fields deliberately
  not forwarded are named with their reason.
- Every §9.4 state has an entry and an exit; the one unreachable transition is marked
  unreachable and traced to F4 and P2.
- Every finding F1–F6 has a named remedy in §8.3; every assumption A1–A7 has a verification
  owner or an invariance argument.

